/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b0efdf32801874ff6ad6af337dfd37977788fadde3dd9557fc2c8bd8766293e"} {"question" "q-82716" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 19 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 22 & 19 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}22 & 19 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}22 & 19 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b0efdf32801874ff6ad6af337dfd37977788fadde3dd9557fc2c8bd8766293e"} {"question" "q-82716" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9e37c1aea9388cdbe89841a8e10d9954c6526a32ace1084811f786b588da9146"} {"question" "q-82716" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b0efdf32801874ff6ad6af337dfd37977788fadde3dd9557fc2c8bd8766293e"} {"question" "q-82716" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b0efdf32801874ff6ad6af337dfd37977788fadde3dd9557fc2c8bd8766293e"} {"question" "q-82716" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b0efdf32801874ff6ad6af337dfd37977788fadde3dd9557fc2c8bd8766293e"} {"question" "q-82618" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b0efdf32801874ff6ad6af337dfd37977788fadde3dd9557fc2c8bd8766293e"} {"question" "q-82618" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 10 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 18 & -29 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}18 & -29 \\\\ -5 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}18 & -29 \\\\ -5 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b0efdf32801874ff6ad6af337dfd37977788fadde3dd9557fc2c8bd8766293e"} {"question" "q-82618" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9e37c1aea9388cdbe89841a8e10d9954c6526a32ace1084811f786b588da9146"} {"question" "q-82618" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b0efdf32801874ff6ad6af337dfd37977788fadde3dd9557fc2c8bd8766293e"} {"question" "q-82618" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ -29\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b0efdf32801874ff6ad6af337dfd37977788fadde3dd9557fc2c8bd8766293e"} {"question" "q-82618" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b0efdf32801874ff6ad6af337dfd37977788fadde3dd9557fc2c8bd8766293e"} {"question" "q-113708" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given above. Find $[x]_{C}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b0efdf32801874ff6ad6af337dfd37977788fadde3dd9557fc2c8bd8766293e"} {"question" "q-113708" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b0efdf32801874ff6ad6af337dfd37977788fadde3dd9557fc2c8bd8766293e"} {"question" "q-113708" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9e37c1aea9388cdbe89841a8e10d9954c6526a32ace1084811f786b588da9146"} {"question" "q-113708" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b0efdf32801874ff6ad6af337dfd37977788fadde3dd9557fc2c8bd8766293e"} {"question" "q-113708" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b0efdf32801874ff6ad6af337dfd37977788fadde3dd9557fc2c8bd8766293e"} {"question" "q-113708" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b0efdf32801874ff6ad6af337dfd37977788fadde3dd9557fc2c8bd8766293e"} {"question" "q-113706" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given above. Find $[x]_{C}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b0efdf32801874ff6ad6af337dfd37977788fadde3dd9557fc2c8bd8766293e"} {"question" "q-113706" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -6 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 7 \\\\ 5 & -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 7 \\\\ 5 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b0efdf32801874ff6ad6af337dfd37977788fadde3dd9557fc2c8bd8766293e"} {"question" "q-113706" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9e37c1aea9388cdbe89841a8e10d9954c6526a32ace1084811f786b588da9146"} {"question" "q-113706" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b0efdf32801874ff6ad6af337dfd37977788fadde3dd9557fc2c8bd8766293e"} {"question" "q-113706" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b0efdf32801874ff6ad6af337dfd37977788fadde3dd9557fc2c8bd8766293e"} {"question" "q-113706" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b0efdf32801874ff6ad6af337dfd37977788fadde3dd9557fc2c8bd8766293e"} {"question" "e-4838" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$ and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ are given below. What is $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{C}}?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/assets/0dce1fb65e0b82525b6d461b3641b10eb6d3641f590d46af6fa4af9f2e020dc6.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/example-4838.json" "file_sha256" "6dc359776b6dbb934adc3096f4d5047cf2aaee04eb48f8954a070826591be060"} {"question" "e-4838" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C},$ we find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to $\\mathcal{C}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 \\\\ 5 & -8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/example-4838.json" "file_sha256" "6dc359776b6dbb934adc3096f4d5047cf2aaee04eb48f8954a070826591be060"} {"question" "e-4840" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ to $\\mathcal{C} = \\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\}$ is given below. Find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ given that $\\mathbf{x} =2\\mathbf{c}_1-6\\mathbf{c}_2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/assets/acdc98dc5a658e7c945dca00c1a1c0a73d87e3dc98db8071a534a4fd7f215c90.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/example-4840.json" "file_sha256" "2f887f558920797d7ff61f73da663fd379915f736dac53323d8d72361f25754c"} {"question" "e-4840" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small\\mathcal{C}\\to\\mathcal{B}} [\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{C}} .\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{C}\\to\\mathcal{B}}$ from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-4)(-4)-6\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $\\mathbf{x} = 2 \\mathbf{c}_1-6\\mathbf{c}_2,$ we obtain\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/assets/883431c76485c9fd706fe79c2bf3936946cfb0dba602ebd0df77d6cc9e800665.png)\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/example-4840.json" "file_sha256" "2f887f558920797d7ff61f73da663fd379915f736dac53323d8d72361f25754c"} {"question" "e-4841" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}$ are given below. Find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/assets/0dcc93449ac1d14e51850e4a74f639aeb3acd21b720ba2b9deeb2208d9fffeb4.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/example-4841.json" "file_sha256" "24bf66197e7bd6e4b6b8ac532025387408e97be41ba9b8c0d056632170444f88"} {"question" "e-4841" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small\\mathcal{S}\\to\\mathcal{B}} [\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{S}} .\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}.$ As a result, we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/assets/50bbd2dd0fa1c0732a01d07c2614ab15549272fcc0b7d686651f0464b3b50f39.png)\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{S}\\to\\mathcal{B}}$ from $\\mathcal{S}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{S}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 10\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 10-3\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/example-4841.json" "file_sha256" "24bf66197e7bd6e4b6b8ac532025387408e97be41ba9b8c0d056632170444f88"} {"question" "e-7276" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ are given below. Find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{C}}.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/assets/c6c10ed068cfde656d178c98eb4af979563efc82d589f750b8fa92973f362e2a.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/example-7276.json" "file_sha256" "3d66f5ade83467f6ff36f262096641d39bbb08f8d6405e0d1c27481659f0d71a"} {"question" "e-7276" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ and $\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\},$ respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$\n\nSo, we need to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/assets/034a2a4079120d719f9061c339899f9aed9b50a765200291ca15c500ef647a70.png)\n\nReducing the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 13 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 11 & 15 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}}$ is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/assets/8d07812ea170bcbb56264468f4cfd0fa8b88cf4f68f9b50d35f61e9e33de45c3.png)\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{C}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}11 & 15 \\\\ -5 & -7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/example-7276.json" "file_sha256" "3d66f5ade83467f6ff36f262096641d39bbb08f8d6405e0d1c27481659f0d71a"}] "usage" {"e-7276" [] "e-4838" [] "e-4841" [] "q-113606" [] "q-36503" [] "e-4840" []} "basis" 2179 "retractions" [[17592186124910 :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given below. What is $[x]_{B}$?\n\n$P_{B → C} = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}, [x]_{C} = \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186124223 :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$ and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ are given below. What is $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{C}}$?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 \\\\ 5 & -8\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186124223 :question/worked-solution "Given the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$, we find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to $\\mathcal{C}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[x]_{C} &= P_{B → C}[x]_{B} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 \\\\ 5 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186124224 :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ to $\\mathcal{C} = \\{\\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\}$ is given below. Find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ given that $\\mathbf{x} =2\\mathbf{c}_1-6\\mathbf{c}_2$.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186124224 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small\\mathcal{C}\\to\\mathcal{B}} [\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{C}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{C}\\to\\mathcal{B}}$ from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}$. To do that, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{C → B} &= P_{B → C}^{-1} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}^{-1} \\\\\n&= (1)/((-4)(-4) - 6 \\cdot 3)\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -3 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = 2 \\mathbf{c}_1-6\\mathbf{c}_2\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[x]_{B} &= P_{C → B}[x]_{C} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186124225 :question/problem "The basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}$ are given below. Find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186124225 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small\\mathcal{S}\\to\\mathcal{B}} [\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{S}}\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}$. As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{S}\\to\\mathcal{B}}$ from $\\mathcal{S}$ to $\\mathcal{B}$. To do that, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{S}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{S → B} &= P_{B → S}^{-1} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 10\\end{bmatrix}^{-1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{1 \\cdot 10 - 3 \\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[x]_{B} &= P_{S → B}[x]_{S} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186124302 :question/problem "The bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ are given below. Find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{C}}$.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186124302 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ as $\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ and $\\{\\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$.\n\nSo, we need to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$. We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n$\\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid; c_{1}\\begin{vmatrix}c_{2} & b_{1}\\end{vmatrix}b_{2}; \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid$\n\nReducing the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 13 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + (-6)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -7\\end{bmatrix} & R_{1}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{1} + (-2)R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 11 & 15 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}}$ is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 15 \\\\ -5 & -7\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{C}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[x]_{C} &= P_{B → C}[x]_{B} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}11 & 15 \\\\ -5 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186122483 17592186122484 17592186122485 17592186122486 17592186122487 17592186294464 17592186294465 17592186294466 17592186294467 17592186294468] "immutable_fields" [[17592186122482 :answer-field/correct] [17592186122482 :answer-field/id] [17592186122482 :answer-field/key] [17592186122482 :answer-field/type] [17592186294463 :answer-field/correct] [17592186294463 :answer-field/id] [17592186294463 :answer-field/key] [17592186294463 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
